==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|recreate}}, from the participle stem of {{der|en|la|recreō|recreāre|to restore}}, from {{m|la|re-||re-}} + {{m|la|creō|creāre|to create}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹɛk.ɹi.eɪt/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-recreate1.wav|a=Southern England}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To give new [[life]], [[energy]] or [[encouragement]] (to); to [[refresh]], [[enliven]].
#* {{RQ:Du Fresnoy Dryden Painting|passage=Painters, when they work on white grounds, place before them colours mixed with blue and green, to '''recreate''' their eyes, white wearying {{...}} the sight more than any.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1688|author=w:Henry More|title=Divine Dialogues
|passage=These ripe fruit{{...}} '''recreate''' the nostrils with their aromatick scent.}}
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy| edition=2nd|part=II|section=5| member=1| subsection=v| passage=Odoraments to smell to, of rose-water, violet flowers, balm, rose-cakes, vinegar, etc., do much '''recreate''' the brains and spirits {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|reflexive}} To [[enjoy]] or entertain oneself.
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd|II|ii|3
|passage=In Italy, though they bide in cities in winter, which is more gentlemanlike, all the summer they come abroad to their country-houses, to '''recreate''' themselves.}}
#* {{RQ:Taylor Holy Living
|passage=St. John, who '''recreated''' himself with sporting with a tame partridge}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To take [[recreation]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2004|journal=Forbes|volume=173|issue=4-9|page=156
|passage=Phonecams are proliferating like mad, their tiny eyes fuzzily probing so many corners of public and private life that they have begun to alter how people communicate and '''recreate'''.}}

